%I A398321 #18 Oct 29 2026 21:03:00
%S A398321 1,4,5,7,10,12,14
%N A398321 Maximum size of a set S of points of the n X n grid such that any two distinct rectangles with all four corners at grid points (tilted rectangles included) have distinct sets of corners outside S.
%C A398321 Main diagonal of A397315 (for n >= 2).
%C A398321 a(5) = 10 is attained by exactly 8 sets, which are the images of one set under the symmetries of the square (see A399580).
%C A398321 a(6) = 12 was found by an integer program and two SAT encodings, and a(7) = 14 by two SAT encodings. Both upper bounds have checked LRAT certificates (see the Pemmasani link).
%H A398321 Peter Kagey, <a href="https://peterkagey.com/problems/001/">Open Problem Collection, Problem 001</a>.
%H A398321 Arjun Pemmasani, <a href="https://github.com/apemm/Kagey-Problems/blob/main/kagey-problems/problem001/jis/pemmasani.pdf">Maximum Blackouts That Keep Every Lattice Rectangle Identifiable</a>, preprint, 2026.
%H A398321 Arjun Pemmasani, <a href="https://github.com/apemm/Kagey-Problems/tree/main/kagey-problems/problem001">Solvers, exhaustive verification, and pre-registration ledger for Problem 001</a> (GitHub repository).
%F A398321 a(n) >= 2*n - 1.
%F A398321 a(n) <= n + A002620(n) for n >= 3.
%F A398321 a(n) >= c*n^(6/5)/(1+log(n))^(2/5) for some c > 0 and all large n, so a(n) - 2*n tends to infinity. Also a(n) = O(n^(4/3)) (see the Pemmasani link).
%e A398321 a(3) = 5. The five points marked 1 form such a set, and no six points do (rows numbered from the top):
%e A398321   1 1 1
%e A398321   1 0 0
%e A398321   1 0 0
%Y A398321 Main diagonal of A397315.
%Y A398321 Cf. A002620, A085582, A399580.
%K A398321 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A398321 1,2
%A A398321 _Arjun Pemmasani_, Oct 22 2026
